## I. THE WEARABLE AND “ALWAYS-ON COMPANION” DELUSION

Across both consumer technology and the open-source hardware community, a dangerous architectural consensus has formed: the belief that as long as an artificial intelligence model runs locally on your own private, air-gapped hardware (`Node_Local`) with zero network egress to external corporate peers (`Node_Ext`), it is completely safe—and desirable—to grant that local model 24/7 continuous telemetry over your life. Developers are racing to build always-on microphone pendants, continuous screen-recording daemons (“local Recall”), biometric wearables, and neural interfaces that feed every spoken word, facial micro-expression, heart-rate fluctuation, and keystroke cadence into a local weight matrix so the machine can “know you completely.”

Evaluated through first-principles cybernetic control theory and zero-trust security (`OPSTACK-KERNEL-v2.2` Invariants 4, 17, 24, 25, and 26), granting even a 100% air-gapped local model continuous observability over your biological state is a catastrophic vulnerability. **Zero-trust does not begin at the router firewall between your house and the internet; zero-trust begins at the physical sensory air-gap between your 20-Watt biological brain (`Node_Bio`) and your own local silicon (`Node_Local`).**

